@anon.879c592214345ca999bafaa324406990.791 = private unnamed_addr constant [1931 x i8] c"## What it does\0AChecks for `async` function definitions with `timeout` parameters.\0A\0A## Why is this bad?\0ARather than implementing asynchronous timeout behavior manually, prefer\0Abuilt-in timeout functionality, such as `asyncio.timeout`, `trio.fail_after`,\0Aor `anyio.move_on_after`, among others.\0A\0AThis rule is highly opinionated to enforce a design pattern\0Acalled [\22structured concurrency\22] that allows for\0A`async` functions to be oblivious to timeouts,\0Ainstead letting callers to handle the logic with a context manager.\0A\0A## Details\0A\0AThis rule attempts to detect which async framework your code is using\0Aby analysing the imports in the file it's checking. If it sees an\0A`anyio` import in your code, it will assume `anyio` is your framework\0Aof choice; if it sees a `trio` import, it will assume `trio`; if it\0Asees neither, it will assume `asyncio`. `asyncio.timeout` was added\0Ain Python 3.11, so if `asyncio` is detected as the framework being used,\0Athis rule will be ignored when your configured [`target-version`] is set\0Ato less than Python 3.11.\0A\0AFor functions that wrap `asyncio.timeout`, `trio.fail_after` or\0A`anyio.move_on_after`, false positives from this rule can be avoided\0Aby using a different parameter name.\0A\0A## Example\0A\0A```python\0Aasync def long_running_task(timeout): ...\0A\0A\0Aasync def main():\0A    await long_running_task(timeout=2)\0A```\0A\0AUse instead:\0A\0A```python\0Aasync def long_running_task(): ...\0A\0A\0Aasync def main():\0A    async with asyncio.timeout(2):\0A        await long_running_task()\0A```\0A\0A## References\0A- [`asyncio` timeouts](https://docs.python.org/3/library/asyncio-task.html#timeouts)\0A- [`anyio` timeouts](https://anyio.readthedocs.io/en/stable/cancellation.html)\0A- [`trio` timeouts](https://trio.readthedocs.io/en/stable/reference-core.html#cancellation-and-timeouts)\0A\0A[\22structured concurrency\22]: https://vorpus.org/blog/some-thoughts-on-asynchronous-api-design-in-a-post-asyncawait-world/#timeouts-and-cancellation\0A", align 1
