@anon.879c592214345ca999bafaa324406990.893 = private unnamed_addr constant [1327 x i8] c"## What it does\0AChecks for `@overload` function definitions that contain a docstring.\0A\0A## Why is this bad?\0AThe `@overload` decorator is used to define multiple compatible signatures\0Afor a given function, to support type-checking. A series of `@overload`\0Adefinitions should be followed by a single non-decorated definition that\0Acontains the implementation of the function.\0A\0A`@overload` function definitions should not contain a docstring; instead,\0Athe docstring should be placed on the non-decorated definition that contains\0Athe implementation.\0A\0A## Example\0A\0A```python\0Afrom typing import overload\0A\0A\0A@overload\0Adef factorial(n: int) -> int:\0A    \22\22\22Return the factorial of n.\22\22\22\0A\0A\0A@overload\0Adef factorial(n: float) -> float:\0A    \22\22\22Return the factorial of n.\22\22\22\0A\0A\0Adef factorial(n):\0A    \22\22\22Return the factorial of n.\22\22\22\0A\0A\0Afactorial.__doc__  # \22Return the factorial of n.\22\0A```\0A\0AUse instead:\0A\0A```python\0Afrom typing import overload\0A\0A\0A@overload\0Adef factorial(n: int) -> int: ...\0A\0A\0A@overload\0Adef factorial(n: float) -> float: ...\0A\0A\0Adef factorial(n):\0A    \22\22\22Return the factorial of n.\22\22\22\0A\0A\0Afactorial.__doc__  # \22Return the factorial of n.\22\0A```\0A\0A## References\0A- [PEP 257 \E2\80\93 Docstring Conventions](https://peps.python.org/pep-0257/)\0A- [Python documentation: `typing.overload`](https://docs.python.org/3/library/typing.html#typing.overload)\0A", align 1
